    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 771002001,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "asbuilt-20260803-c2-write-gate",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 60,
      "y": 9700,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 980,
      "height": 640,
      "seed": 771002001,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 1,
      "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-03 (run 20260801-171149, slice C2) - THE WRITE GATE, AND ONE EGRESS TRUTH ON THIS RAIL.\nNEW MODULE api/src/integrations/action-consent.ts (tier 3, integrations/): the durable approval store for\n`mutates` actions. Sibling of automation/consent.ts, NOT a call into it - module direction runs\nintegrations/ -> (nothing in automation/), which is why action-executor.ts reaches the engine only through an\ninjected seam. Same tested semantics (90-day TTL, org+user scope, a row with no expiry reads as EXPIRED);\nextracting the shared core down into security/ is the journaled consolidation, not this slice's business.\nNO NEW EDGE out of the tier for the gate itself: integrations/ -> data/stores (approved_integration_actions).\nENFORCEMENT POINT: executeUserIntegrationAction, before the credential lookup. Deliberately the EXECUTOR and\nnot any route, because the executor is where all four rails meet - the capability route, the automation\nengine's `integration` step, the listener supervisor's poll tick and the agent tool seam. A gate on a route\nwould be a gate on one of four doors. Consequence, chosen not tolerated: an unapproved write on an integration\nthat is not even connected answers awaiting_consent rather than not_connected.\nFAIL-CLOSED: only a literal `mutates: false` is a read. config.json is parsed, never schema-validated, so\nabsent / \"false\" / 0 / null are all writes. The approval key is (org, user, integrationKey, actionName, SHAPE),\nwhere SHAPE hashes what the action actually does - backing, transport, method, URL and templates - the direct\nanalogue of command-shape.ts. Re-authoring an action therefore does not inherit the answer a human gave the\nold one, which is criterion 6's \"before an authored one persists as executable\".\nCLOSED IN THE SAME SLICE (B2's named second credential rail): action-executor.ts's HTTP path had NO origin\nbinding - it loaded and decrypted the owner's credentials and dialled whatever host the package named, behind\nan SSRF guard that permits every public host. B2's review proved it with a LOCKED item: the request still went\nout. It now resolves the same Cofre item scope the automation api_call seam uses (integrationOriginScope +\ndeclaredOriginsForIntegration, both from credential-cofre.ts - no second derivation), so lock = revoke is true\non this rail too. NEW EDGES: integrations/action-executor.ts -> cofre/ (item scope) and -> security/\norigin-binding (assertOriginAllowed). The one unbound case is named, not hidden: a package whose baseUrl is a\ncredential template ({{api_base}}) declares no literal host, mints no item, and keeps the pre-C2 posture.\nALSO: a provider rotation (Zoho grant_code -> refresh_token) now writes BOTH stores through service.ts\nshadowCredentials - before C2 it wrote the legacy column only and the shadow went stale from the first\nrotation, which would have handed a burnt grant code back at the 2026-08-15 cutover.\nWEB: components/integrations/action-consent-dialog.tsx - the dashboard half. It states the integration, the\naction and the real destination, because a confirm dialog that does not say what it is confirming is not\nconsent. The three routes behind it are auth 'user', never 'user-or-key': a key-bearing agent refused at the\ngate must not be able to approve itself and retry.",
      "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-03 (run 20260801-171149, slice C2) - THE WRITE GATE, AND ONE EGRESS TRUTH ON THIS RAIL.\nNEW MODULE api/src/integrations/action-consent.ts (tier 3, integrations/): the durable approval store for\n`mutates` actions. Sibling of automation/consent.ts, NOT a call into it - module direction runs\nintegrations/ -> (nothing in automation/), which is why action-executor.ts reaches the engine only through an\ninjected seam. Same tested semantics (90-day TTL, org+user scope, a row with no expiry reads as EXPIRED);\nextracting the shared core down into security/ is the journaled consolidation, not this slice's business.\nNO NEW EDGE out of the tier for the gate itself: integrations/ -> data/stores (approved_integration_actions).\nENFORCEMENT POINT: executeUserIntegrationAction, before the credential lookup. Deliberately the EXECUTOR and\nnot any route, because the executor is where all four rails meet - the capability route, the automation\nengine's `integration` step, the listener supervisor's poll tick and the agent tool seam. A gate on a route\nwould be a gate on one of four doors. Consequence, chosen not tolerated: an unapproved write on an integration\nthat is not even connected answers awaiting_consent rather than not_connected.\nFAIL-CLOSED: only a literal `mutates: false` is a read. config.json is parsed, never schema-validated, so\nabsent / \"false\" / 0 / null are all writes. The approval key is (org, user, integrationKey, actionName, SHAPE),\nwhere SHAPE hashes what the action actually does - backing, transport, method, URL and templates - the direct\nanalogue of command-shape.ts. Re-authoring an action therefore does not inherit the answer a human gave the\nold one, which is criterion 6's \"before an authored one persists as executable\".\nCLOSED IN THE SAME SLICE (B2's named second credential rail): action-executor.ts's HTTP path had NO origin\nbinding - it loaded and decrypted the owner's credentials and dialled whatever host the package named, behind\nan SSRF guard that permits every public host. B2's review proved it with a LOCKED item: the request still went\nout. It now resolves the same Cofre item scope the automation api_call seam uses (integrationOriginScope +\ndeclaredOriginsForIntegration, both from credential-cofre.ts - no second derivation), so lock = revoke is true\non this rail too. NEW EDGES: integrations/action-executor.ts -> cofre/ (item scope) and -> security/\norigin-binding (assertOriginAllowed). The one unbound case is named, not hidden: a package whose baseUrl is a\ncredential template ({{api_base}}) declares no literal host, mints no item, and keeps the pre-C2 posture.\nALSO: a provider rotation (Zoho grant_code -> refresh_token) now writes BOTH stores through service.ts\nshadowCredentials - before C2 it wrote the legacy column only and the shadow went stale from the first\nrotation, which would have handed a burnt grant code back at the 2026-08-15 cutover.\nWEB: components/integrations/action-consent-dialog.tsx - the dashboard half. It states the integration, the\naction and the real destination, because a confirm dialog that does not say what it is confirming is not\nconsent. The three routes behind it are auth 'user', never 'user-or-key': a key-bearing agent refused at the\ngate must not be able to approve itself and retry.",
