      "locked": false,
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 3,
      "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-03 (run 20260801-171149, slice D1) - THE PUBLIC INTEGRATIONS CAPABILITY SURFACE.\n\nNEW api/src/integrations/integration-capability.ts (tier 3, integrations/). The capability CORE behind\ntwo new public routes. It imports definition-registry (A2's tenant-scoped resolve), service\n(findConfigForOwner), action-executor (executeUserIntegrationAction) and the READ side of\naction-consent (actionRequiresConsent / describeAction / liveApprovalFor). It imports data/activity for\nthe per-execute audit row. It does NOT import automation/ - the automation-backed handler arrives as an\ninjected seam, exactly as it does for the other rails.\n\nroutes/integrations.ts now has TWO ADMISSION TIERS, and the order of registration is load-bearing:\n  1  GET /                                        requireUserOrApiKey   (the D1 list FLIP)\n  2  /active /configs /refresh /definitions/...    requireAuth  (literal first segment - must precede :key)\n  3  GET /:key                                    requireUserOrApiKey   NEW\n     POST /:key/actions/:actionName/execute       requireUserOrApiKey   NEW\n  4  r.use(requireAuth)  <- the blanket; EVERY remaining :key route, incl. all three C2 consent routes\n\nTHE WRITE GATE IS INHERITED, NOT COPIED. The execute route calls executeUserIntegrationAction and meets\nC2's checkActionConsent INSIDE it - the same funnel as the agent/automation seam, the listener supervisor\nand the automation integration step. There are now exactly THREE inventoried executor call sites\n(server.ts x2, integration-capability.ts x1) and the consent module is imported by the executor and by\nthe surfaces that ANSWER the prompt, never by a rail that merely calls actions.\n\nAND THE KEY CANNOT ANSWER ITS OWN PROMPT. All three consent descriptors stay auth:'user' (C2) and sit\nBELOW the blanket, so a gateway key refused at the gate gets 401 on POST .../approval.\n\nCONTRACT: shared/src/integrations.ts gains IntegrationCapability / IntegrationCapabilityAction /\nExecuteIntegrationActionRequest / ExecuteIntegrationActionResponse / IntegrationActionConsentRequest +\nthe two param schemas; integrations.list flips 'user' -> 'user-or-key'. Three integrations operations are\ntherefore now in docs/openapi/cortex.v1.json and in the generated clients/cortex-cli client.",
      "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-03 (run 20260801-171149, slice D1) - THE PUBLIC INTEGRATIONS CAPABILITY SURFACE.\n\nNEW api/src/integrations/integration-capability.ts (tier 3, integrations/). The capability CORE behind\ntwo new public routes. It imports definition-registry (A2's tenant-scoped resolve), service\n(findConfigForOwner), action-executor (executeUserIntegrationAction) and the READ side of\naction-consent (actionRequiresConsent / describeAction / liveApprovalFor). It imports data/activity for\nthe per-execute audit row. It does NOT import automation/ - the automation-backed handler arrives as an\ninjected seam, exactly as it does for the other rails.\n\nroutes/integrations.ts now has TWO ADMISSION TIERS, and the order of registration is load-bearing:\n  1  GET /                                        requireUserOrApiKey   (the D1 list FLIP)\n  2  /active /configs /refresh /definitions/...    requireAuth  (literal first segment - must precede :key)\n  3  GET /:key                                    requireUserOrApiKey   NEW\n     POST /:key/actions/:actionName/execute       requireUserOrApiKey   NEW\n  4  r.use(requireAuth)  <- the blanket; EVERY remaining :key route, incl. all three C2 consent routes\n\nTHE WRITE GATE IS INHERITED, NOT COPIED. The execute route calls executeUserIntegrationAction and meets\nC2's checkActionConsent INSIDE it - the same funnel as the agent/automation seam, the listener supervisor\nand the automation integration step. There are now exactly THREE inventoried executor call sites\n(server.ts x2, integration-capability.ts x1) and the consent module is imported by the executor and by\nthe surfaces that ANSWER the prompt, never by a rail that merely calls actions.\n\nAND THE KEY CANNOT ANSWER ITS OWN PROMPT. All three consent descriptors stay auth:'user' (C2) and sit\nBELOW the blanket, so a gateway key refused at the gate gets 401 on POST .../approval.\n\nCONTRACT: shared/src/integrations.ts gains IntegrationCapability / IntegrationCapabilityAction /\nExecuteIntegrationActionRequest / ExecuteIntegrationActionResponse / IntegrationActionConsentRequest +\nthe two param schemas; integrations.list flips 'user' -> 'user-or-key'. Three integrations operations are\ntherefore now in docs/openapi/cortex.v1.json and in the generated clients/cortex-cli client.",
      "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-03 (run 20260801-171149, slice D1) - THE PUBLIC INTEGRATIONS CAPABILITY SURFACE.\n\nNEW api/src/integrations/integration-capability.ts (tier 3, integrations/). The capability CORE behind\ntwo new public routes. It imports definition-registry (A2's tenant-scoped resolve), service\n(findConfigForOwner), action-executor (executeUserIntegrationAction) and the READ side of\naction-consent (actionRequiresConsent / describeAction / liveApprovalFor). It imports data/activity for\nthe per-execute audit row. It does NOT import automation/ - the automation-backed handler arrives as an\ninjected seam, exactly as it does for the other rails.\n\nroutes/integrations.ts now has TWO ADMISSION TIERS, and the order of registration is load-bearing:\n  1  GET /                                        requireUserOrApiKey   (the D1 list FLIP)\n  2  /active /configs /refresh /definitions/...    requireAuth  (literal first segment - must precede :key)\n  3  GET /:key                                    requireUserOrApiKey   NEW\n     POST /:key/actions/:actionName/execute       requireUserOrApiKey   NEW\n  4  r.use(requireAuth)  <- the blanket; EVERY remaining :key route, incl. all three C2 consent routes\n\nTHE WRITE GATE IS INHERITED, NOT COPIED. The execute route calls executeUserIntegrationAction and meets\nC2's checkActionConsent INSIDE it - the same funnel as the agent/automation seam, the listener supervisor\nand the automation integration step. There are now exactly THREE inventoried executor call sites\n(server.ts x2, integration-capability.ts x1) and the consent module is imported by the executor and by\nthe surfaces that ANSWER the prompt, never by a rail that merely calls actions.\n\nAND THE KEY CANNOT ANSWER ITS OWN PROMPT. All three consent descriptors stay auth:'user' (C2) and sit\nBELOW the blanket, so a gateway key refused at the gate gets 401 on POST .../approval.\n\nCONTRACT: shared/src/integrations.ts gains IntegrationCapability / IntegrationCapabilityAction /\nExecuteIntegrationActionRequest / ExecuteIntegrationActionResponse / IntegrationActionConsentRequest +\nthe two param schemas; integrations.list flips 'user' -> 'user-or-key'. Three integrations operations are\ntherefore now in docs/openapi/cortex.v1.json and in the generated clients/cortex-cli client.",
